<p><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" src="https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/2026-09-24-black-and-white-1.png" alt="expedient chess puzzle 1" width="385" height="389" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-74278" srcset="https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/2026-09-24-black-and-white-1.png 385w, https://www.futilitycloset.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/2026-09-24-black-and-white-1-300x303.png 300w" sizes="(max-width: 385px) 100vw, 385px" /></p>
<p>I don&#8217;t know who devised this; I found it in <a href="https://archive.org/details/batsfordchesspuz0000bard/page/148/mode/2up"><em>The Batsford Chess Puzzle Book</em></a> (2002). Black has only a king and a bishop, while White still has all 16 of his men. But Black threatens to take White&#8217;s knight, giving mate immediately. If each side uses every legal expedient, what will be the outcome? For a start, White can stave off mate with 1. Bh2!</p>

